dren and i can think of my own  example i was only interested in  communicating i was not interested in  being corrected i was not interested in  i was not concerned about how i sounded  i didn't notice if i made mistakes i  gradually picked up on how things were  supposed to be said  so that the children the biggest  advantage  from the point of view of an attitude  the biggest advantage they have is  they're not worried about how they sound  they only want to communicate so if we  as adults  were able to have a similar attitude to  just focus on communication and not  worry about how we sound we would do a  lot better and we would probably become  more attuned to how the language is used  and naturally pick it up so that's one  difference the children are only  interested in communicating  they get a lot more exposure they're  only interested in communicating but the  process is the same now obviously adults  have certain advantages no i should say  before we move to advantages adults have  another disadvantage and that is that  their brain has already kind of  coalesced around  one language so this now is sort of  interference insofar as learning a  second language but that needn't be a  big obstacle as was the case with my  moving from swedish to english if we get  enough exposure  however if we allow our first language  to influence us  then  it will and i'll use the example of my  father my father spoke english very well  lived in canada for 40 years  uh  30 years actually until he passed away  and he was forever influenced by  the way he felt  english should be pronounced  as an example there's a province in  canada called nova scotia  s-c-o-t-i-a nova scotia that's how it's  said in english to him it was always  nova  scotia to him he felt it should be nova  scotia because if he were at all  interested in pronouncing it the way  native speakers pronounced it he would  have said nova scotia and we see this  all the time that we are influenced by  how words are pronounced in our own  language and i've used this example that  a spanish speaker might say ward or  guard instead of word  and and kind of refuses to notice that  word bird heard uh third that all that  the o and the i and the e a vowel sound  are all pronounced the same so if we are  if we want to improve our pronunciation  if we pay attention to what we're  listening to if we get enough exposure  we can actually get quite close to  native-like pronunciation but very often  in the case of the adult there is that  interference from  his or her native language  uh the other thing is  if we now talk advantages of course that  the adult has a much larger vocabulary  in his or her native language has a  broader life experience and therefore  can learn  a lot of vocabulary and can can become  capable of expressing quite um you know  sophisticated or complicated concepts in  a new language faster than  a child  and i'll use my again my own example you  know within a year of learning check i  can read books on history i can  understand political interviews i don't  think a four-year-old  child after one year of czech would  so those are some of the differences but  the the actual process of learning i'm  quite convinced  uh is very similar now again with regard  to grammar an adult may spend more time  reading some grammar rules and and i i'm  not an extreme crashonite i think that  reading grammar rules and studying  grammar tables  does help to some extent  it helps us notice things in the  language just as being corrected which  krashen has demonstrated in in in  numerous research articles has very  little effect and i'm sure that that's  correct but in my experience it has some  effect it's part of those little things  that help us notice  massive exposure helps us notice because  again the first time through you don't  notice a thing and by  repeatedly listening and reading you  start to notice more and more so  i still believe that the essential  process of learning  is the same  in other words it's it's largely through  exposure massive exposure exposure to  things of interest  and that willingness to engage in  meaningful communication  and and if if we as adults had as much  exposure as the child  has and had the same attitude of of not  worrying about how we sound then i think  probably adults can learn uh  maybe not quite as well when it comes to  pronunciation because our minds our  brains are more  uh  sort of rigidly  uh  sort of predetermined now  to process the native language whereas  the child is more flexible in that  regard but we can learn  by and large somewhat different to  different degrees we can be as  successful better on the vocabulary side  maybe less  capable on the pronunciation side but  adults can learn as well but i do  believe that essentially essentially the  process is the same  so and if i can figure out how i can  make an imovie out of my uh powerpoint  or in fact keynote presentation  i will do so uh first in russian and  then in other languages so thank you for  listening bye for now
 

